UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 36, Lesson 3: Asking good research questions', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Good research questions are open enough to need investigation but clear enough to answer with facts; closed yes/no questions are often too thin.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRewrite \"Do shadows exist?\" as a stronger research question.\n\nFEEDBACK\nExample: Why do shadows change length during the day?\n\nCHECK\nQuestions that already contain the full answer are not research questions.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite ten research questions on two topics. Label each open or closed. Keep your three strongest open questions and justify why they are researchable.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Good research questions are open enough to need investigation but clear enough to answer with facts; closed yes/no questions are often too thin.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and spelling","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-VOCAB-01","EN2-SPELL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write ten research questions on two topics. Label each open or closed. Keep your three strongest open questions and justify why they are researchable. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Example: Why do shadows change length during the day? Reasoning: I used question by attending to open and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Questions that already contain the full answer are not research questions. Correction: Example: Why do shadows change length during the day? Why: the correction restores accurate use of question.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Questions that already contain the full answer are not research questions. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating question on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with question, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Example: Why do shadows change length during the day? This matches the lesson skill for question.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Example: Why do shadows change length during the day?","Questions that already contain the full answer are not research questions.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Rewrite \"Do shadows exist?\" as a stronger research question. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Questions that already contain the full answer are not research questions. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Questions that already contain the full answer are not research questions.","Good research questions are open enough to need investigation but clear enough to answer with facts; closed yes/no questions are often too thin.","Example: Why do shadows change length during the day?","Careful use of open and explain why it matters with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising question?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named open and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the open and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"A learner is practising question. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries question.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show question and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Questions that already contain the full answer are not research questions."],"question":"Read: \"After the blackout, Tess lit a candle and read the crumpled note.\" Which choice best uses question from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Questions that already contain the full answer are not research questions.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Example: Why do shadows change length during the day?"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about question?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise open and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Questions that already contain the full answer are not research questions.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about question.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of question to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating question on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Example: Why do shadows change length during the day? Reasoning: I used question by attending to open and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with question, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Questions that already contain the full answer are not research questions. Correction: Example: Why do shadows change length during the day? Why: the correction restores accurate use of question.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w36-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise question.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use question with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify open and explain why it matters.","I can practise closed independently on a new prompt.","I can notice research needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Good research questions are open enough to need investigation but clear enough to answer with facts; closed yes/no questions are often too thin. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Good research questions are open enough to need investigation but clear enough to answer with facts; closed yes/no questions are often too thin.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about question?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThin: \"Are bees cool?\" Stronger: \"How do honeybees communicate the direction of flowers?\"\n\nTRY FIRST\nRewrite \"Do shadows exist?\" as a stronger research question.\n\nFEEDBACK\nExample: Why do shadows change length during the day?","explain":"Good research questions are open enough to need investigation but clear enough to answer with facts; closed yes/no questions are often too thin.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Questions that already contain the full answer are not research questions. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Open research question contrasted with a thin closed one.","caption":"Open questions need investigation.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stronger</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">How do bees share flower direction?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Thin</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Are bees cool?</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Thin: \"Are bees cool?\" Stronger: \"How do honeybees communicate the direction of flowers?\" Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Thin: \"Are bees cool?\" Stronger: \"How do honeybees communicate the direction of flowers?\" Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about question?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThin: \"Are bees cool?\" Stronger: \"How do honeybees communicate the direction of flowers?\" Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Rewritten question ready for investigation.","caption":"Keep questions clear and researchable.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Rewrite</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Why do shadows change length?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Not research</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">question that already holds the full answe</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about question?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nQuestions that already contain the full answer are not research questions. Here is a more productive model: Thin: \"Are bees cool?\" Stronger: \"How do honeybees communicate the direction of flowers?\"\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nExample: Why do shadows change length during the day?","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Questions that already contain the full answer are not research questions. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about question?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nGood research questions are open enough to need investigation but clear enough to answer with facts; closed yes/no questions are often too thin. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 36, Lesson 3: Asking good research questions","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Research project and portfolio","week":36,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nThin: \"Are bees cool?\" Stronger: \"How do honeybees communicate the direction of flowers?\"\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Open research question contrasted with a thin closed one.","caption":"Open questions need investigation.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stronger</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">How do bees share flower direction?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Thin</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Are bees cool?</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Rewritten question ready for investigation.","caption":"Keep questions clear and researchable.","sv